<HelpHead Page="how-to-play/connect" Title="How to play Connect (Shisen-Sho)"
          Description="How to play Connect, also called Shisen-Sho: join matching mahjong tiles with a line of up to two turns. The rules, gravity, board sizes, scoring and tips." />

<article class="help" lang="en">
    <h1>How to play Connect (Shisen-Sho)</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        Connect is a mahjong matching game from Japan, where it's called Shisen-Sho. The tiles lie flat in a single rectangle,
        with no stacking, and two matching tiles can be taken when a line with at most two turns joins them through empty space.
    </p>

    <BoardPicture Connect="true" Alt="A Connect game: a rectangle of mahjong tiles twelve wide and six high, with a red line joining a matching pair" Caption="A small Connect board, 12 tiles by 6. The red line shows a pair that can be joined." />

    <h2>The goal</h2>
    <p>Clear the board by joining matching pairs. When the last pair goes, you've won.</p>

    <h2>The rules</h2>
    <p>Two tiles can be taken together when:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>They're identical.</strong> Each tile matches only its twin.</li>
        <li><strong>A line can join them</strong> that runs only through empty spaces, in straight horizontal and vertical pieces,
            and turns <strong>no more than twice</strong>. The line may run around the outside of the board, along the edge.</li>
    </ul>
    <RuleDiagram Kind="connect" Caption="A straight line, one turn or two turns joins a pair; the line can go round the outside of the board. A pair that needs three or more turns can't be taken yet." />
    <p>
        Unlike Mahjong Solitaire, any tile can be taken in Connect, wherever it lies, as long as its twin can be reached. The puzzle
        is in the paths: every pair you take opens new routes for the tiles around it.
    </p>

    <h2>Gravity</h2>
    <p>
        Gravity is an optional twist. With it on, the tiles close up the gaps that pairs leave behind: with <strong>Down</strong>, the
        tiles above a gap fall into it; with <strong>Left</strong>, the tiles to the right of a gap slide over. The board keeps changing
        shape as you play, so you have to think a move ahead.
    </p>
    <RuleDiagram Kind="gravity" Caption="With gravity down, the tiles above a gap fall to fill it." />
    <p>Each setting, off, down or left, has its own leaderboard.</p>

    <h2>The tiles</h2>
    <p>Connect uses the same tiles as the other games, and each tile matches only its identical twin.</p>
    <TileGuide />

    <h2>Playing on Mahjong Haus</h2>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Choose Connect (Shisen-Sho)</strong> in the settings (the gear in the top bar).</li>
        <li><strong>Choose a board:</strong> small (12×6, 72 tiles), medium (14×8, 112 tiles) or classic (18×8, 144 tiles, the size
            of the original game). Then choose gravity, your tiles and a background.</li>
        <li><strong>Take a pair.</strong> Tap a tile, then its twin. If a path joins them, a line flashes between them and both go.</li>
        <li><strong>Use the tools under the board:</strong> undo and redo, a hint, shuffle, the demo, and pause.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Scoring</h2>
    <p>
        Connect is scored like the other games: points for every tile, pace and speed bonuses, finish bonuses for a quick finish,
        and a no-shuffle bonus. Each board and gravity setting has its own leaderboard.
        <a href="how-to-play/mahjong-solitaire#scoring">See how scoring works.</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Tips</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Start at the edges.</strong> Tiles on the outside can always use the space around the board, so pairs there are
            often easy. Clearing the edge also opens paths into the middle.</li>
        <li><strong>Take pairs that open the most space.</strong> A pair in the middle of a crowded area makes room for many new paths.</li>
        <li><strong>Watch for crossed pairs.</strong> If two pairs block each other (A B A B in a row, say), neither can be joined
            straight across. Clear a way around before the board fills in.</li>
        <li><strong>With gravity, think a move ahead.</strong> Taking a pair moves the tiles above or beside it, which can open a path
            or close one.</li>
        <li><strong>Learn on the small board.</strong> It's quick to clear and shows the patterns well.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <dl>
        <dt>What does Shisen-Sho mean?</dt>
        <dd>It's the game's Japanese name: Shisen-shō means Sichuan Province, in China. The game is also called Four Rivers, Two Corners and Nikakudori.</dd>
        <dt>Can the line cross other tiles?</dt>
        <dd>No. It only passes through empty spaces, or around the outside of the board.</dd>
        <dt>Can every board be cleared?</dt>
        <dd>With Guaranteed winnable path on, yes, though taking pairs in the wrong order can still leave you stuck. Undo, or shuffle the tiles left.</dd>
    </dl>

